  "raw_label": "-1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Dr Akash Bhattacharya vs Delhi Disaster Management Authority & ... on 5 October, 2023 Author: Satish Chandra Sharma Bench: Chief Justice, Sanjeev Narula $~35 * In The High Court Of Delhi At New Delhi + W.P.(C) 9701/2023 Dr Akash Bhattacharya Through: None. ve Delhi Disaster Management Authority & An Through: Mr. Santosh Kumar Gnctd with Mr. Ud Gnctd with Mr. Ar Pradyumn Rao and Sharma, Advocates Coram: Hon'Ble The Chief Justice Hon'Ble Mr. Justice Sanjeev Narula Or % 05.10.2023 1.",
    "facts": "The Petitioner before this Court has filed this present petition as a Public Interest Litigation ('PIL') stating that in the monsoon season this year, on account of flooding in Yamuna River, the water level crossed danger level of 205.33 and large numbers of persons in NCT of Delhi stood displaced. The Petitioner submits that around 25,000 people, apart from livestock, were directly or indirectly affected by the floods. In the prayer clause, the following reliefs are sought: \"1. Direct Reposndents 1 & 2 to ensure essential & quality food supply in all relief camps across Delhi. 2. Direct Respondent No.2 to start a community kitchen at every flood relief camp in Delhi. The authenticity of the order can be re-verified from Delhi High Court Order Portal by scanning the QR code shown above. 3. Direct Respondents 1 & 2 to provide sanitary items for the flood victims in the relief camps including soap, Sanitizer etc. 4. Direct Respondent No.1 & 2 to arrange required light arrangements in every flood relief camp in the State. 5. Direct Respondent 2 to provide a 3-month free ration including spices and basic essentials to prepare food for all victims affected by the flood. 6. Direct Respondent No.2 to conduct a probe on procedural lapses from the side of departments since the day one of Yamuna Flood, 2023. 7. Direct Respondent No. 2 to ensure there shall be no discrimination at relief camps based on caste, creed, religion, language, ethnicity etc. 7. Direct Respondent No. 2 to provide vaccination or other measurements for people in the relief camps against any epidemic, water-borne disease or any other diseases. 8. Direct Respondent No. 2 to sensitize the general public through digital, print and any other available mediums about the precautions that have to be taken in the aftermath of the flood. 9. Direct Respondent No. 2 to prepare for any endemic in the post-flood scenario. 10. Direct Respondent No. 2 to provide essential medical services for women, children and old age people at relief camps against any illness. 11. Direct Respondent No. 2 to make a public call for relief materials and donations for the relief works in the relief camps and rehabilitation of victims. 12. Direct Respondent No. 2 to appoint a nodal officer to conduct survey for the relief and rehabilitation of flood victims in every camp. 13. Direct Respondent No. 2 to provide immediate cash assistance of Rs. 50000 for those who lost their belonging and shelter in the flood. 14. Direct Respondent No. 2 to Provide Text Books and other essential materials and stationaries to all school-college-going students in the relief camp and those who are subjected to the flood. 15 Direct Respondent No. 2 to notify immediate flood as a natural This is a digitally signed order. The Order is downloaded from the DHC Server on 09/10/2023 at 21:12:37 disaster under DMA, Act, 2005.\" 2. Today, there is no appearance on behalf of the Petitioner. We note that this Court issued notice in the matter on 24th July, 2023 and the Government of NCT of Delhi (\"GNCTD\") has filed a detailed status report. To assist the Apex Committee, a \"Central Control Room\" is set up every year from 15th June to 15th October at L.M. Bund, Shastri Nagar, which functions round the clock. 4. Around 27,381 persons were evacuated from flood-affected low-lying areas and a total of 47 relief camps were established across 6 districts of NCT of Delhi. 5. GNCTD's report further states that a financial assistance of Rs. 10,000/- per family was provided to 2223 families after verification, the details of which have also been enclosed along with the status report. GNCTD's report also indicates that not only did the State Government provide relief and aid to flood-affected people, but various NGOs, gurudwaras, and other organisations also participated in relief activities, including distribution of stationery and clothing items.",
